{
  "analysis": "The keyframes come in pairs/triples very close together (e.g., 10.600, 10.633, 10.967, 11.000), likely capturing each character being typed or each screen refresh. 861 keyframes is a lot, but many are duplicates of the same screen state. I need a smarter approach. Since the game has 262 moves in ~190 seconds, and moves are typed character by character, I should OCR keyframes and diff them to find when a complete command appears (when the '>' prompt line changes). However, OCR-ing 861 frames will take ~15 minutes. Let me try a different approach: use ffmpeg to extract at a moderate fps (like 5fps = ~950 frames) and OCR those, or better yet, reduce keyframes by taking only one frame per cluster. But the most efficient approach might be to OCR every Nth keyframe or use the already OCR'd 1fps frames and fill gaps. Let me first look at the full 1fps move list to understand what we have, then decide if we need more precision.",
  "plan": "View the complete moves_raw.txt file and all '>' lines from the 1fps OCR. Then try OCR on a few keyframes to see if they capture intermediate moves better. Based on quality, decide whether to OCR all keyframes or use a hybrid approach.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /app/moves_raw.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "for f in /app/keyframes/key_0001.png /app/keyframes/key_0010.png /app/keyframes/key_0050.png /app/keyframes/key_0100.png /app/keyframes/key_0200.png /app/keyframes/key_0400.png /app/keyframes/key_0600.png /app/keyframes/key_0800.png; do echo \"=== $f ===\"; tesseract \"$f\" stdout 2>/dev/null | tail -8; echo; done\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}
